WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.467 --> 00:00:20.056
Dickon pushed Colin's wheelchair until they came to a spot in the garden filled with flowering trees.

2
00:00:20.057 --> 00:00:23.969
Mary and Dickon worked here and there as Colin watched.

3
00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:32.821
They brought him things to look at: green buds on a twig, a woodpecker's feather, the empty shell of a bird that had hatched.

4
00:00:32.822 --> 00:00:36.922
Then Dickon pushed him slowly around the garden.

5
00:00:36.923 --> 00:00:40.452
"I wonder if we'll see the robin," said Colin.

6
00:00:40.453 --> 00:00:44.061
"Oh, you'll see him soon enough," said Dickon.

7
00:00:44.062 --> 00:00:48.644
"When the eggs hatch, he'll be busy bringing worms to his family."

8
00:00:48.645 --> 00:00:50.995
Dickon pulled out his pipe.

9
00:00:50.996 --> 00:00:54.862
Before he could begin to play, Colin noticed something.

10
00:00:54.863 --> 00:00:57.729
"That's a very old tree over there."

11
00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:02.790
"Yes," Dickon said quietly. He glanced at Mary.

12
00:01:02.791 --> 00:01:07.058
"It looks as if a big branch broke off," said Colin.

13
00:01:07.059 --> 00:01:09.509
"I wonder how that happened."

14
00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:16.043
"It broke a long time ago," said Dickon. "Oh, look, here's the robin."

15
00:01:16.044 --> 00:01:19.754
Colin laughed as he watched the bird flying around.

16
00:01:19.755 --> 00:01:23.494
He forgot all about the tree.

17
00:01:23.495 --> 00:01:29.747
When they talked about it later, Mary told Dickon it was magic that the robin had appeared just then.

18
00:01:29.748 --> 00:01:33.753
They hadn't wanted to tell Colin about the horrible accident.

19
00:01:33.754 --> 00:01:39.598
"Mother thinks Colin's mother sometimes comes back to watch over him," Dickon had said.

20
00:01:39.599 --> 00:01:43.881
"Perhaps she set us to work and told us to bring him here."

21
00:01:43.882 --> 00:01:47.678
Mary wondered whether this could be true.

22
00:01:47.679 --> 00:01:52.835
There was no question, however, that being in the garden was doing Colin good.

23
00:01:52.836 --> 00:01:56.087
He was still gazing around in wonder.

24
00:01:56.088 --> 00:02:01.360
"I'll watch everything grow here," he announced, "and I'll grow too."

25
00:02:01.361 --> 00:02:07.605
"That's true," said Dickon. "We'll have you walking and digging soon."

26
00:02:07.606 --> 00:02:12.089
"Walking! Digging! Will I really do those things?"

27
00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:14.908
Dickon and Mary looked at each other.

28
00:02:14.909 --> 00:02:18.847
Neither of them had ever asked Colin about his legs.

29
00:02:18.848 --> 00:02:22.116
But it was as if he could read their minds.

30
00:02:22.117 --> 00:02:26.805
"Nothing is really wrong with my legs, but they're so thin and weak.

31
00:02:26.806 --> 00:02:31.401
They shake so much that I'm afraid to try to stand."

32
00:02:31.402 --> 00:02:35.010
Both Dickon and Mary breathed a sigh of relief.

33
00:02:35.011 --> 00:02:39.932
"When you stop being afraid," said Dickon, "then you'll stand.

34
00:02:39.933 --> 00:02:42.800
And you'll stop being afraid soon."

35
00:02:42.801 --> 00:02:48.294
They all sat quietly for a while. The sun was dropping lower.

36
00:02:48.295 --> 00:02:52.827
It was that hour of the day when everything stills itself.

37
00:02:52.828 --> 00:03:00.221
So in the midst of this stillness, it was rather alarming when Colin suddenly said, "Who is that man?"

38
00:03:00.222 --> 00:03:06.340
Dickon and Mary scrambled to their feet. "Man?" they cried.

39
00:03:06.341 --> 00:03:09.856
Colin pointed to the wall. "Look!"

40
00:03:09.857 --> 00:03:16.655
They turned around. There was Ben Weatherstaff glaring at them from the top of a ladder.

41
00:03:16.656 --> 00:03:19.739
He shook his fist at Mary.

42
00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:23.075
"I've never liked you!" he shouted.

43
00:03:23.076 --> 00:03:26.551
"You're always poking your nose where it's not wanted.

44
00:03:26.552 --> 00:03:28.905
If it hadn't been for that robin . . ."

45
00:03:28.906 --> 00:03:32.686
"Ben Weatherstaff!" Mary stood below him.

46
00:03:32.687 --> 00:03:35.655
"It was the robin who showed me the way."

47
00:03:35.656 --> 00:03:43.386
At this the old gardener seemed so outraged that Mary thought he was going to scramble down on her side of the wall.

48
00:03:43.387 --> 00:03:48.532
"You're a bad one. Blaming the robin for what you did."

49
00:03:48.533 --> 00:03:53.901
While Ben kept scolding, Colin whispered to Dickon. "Wheel me over there."

50
00:03:53.902 --> 00:03:57.502
Ben's mouth dropped open at the sight of Colin.

51
00:03:57.503 --> 00:04:01.697
"Do you know who I am?" demanded Colin.

52
00:04:01.698 --> 00:04:04.707
Ben stared at the boy in the wheelchair.

53
00:04:04.708 --> 00:04:10.666
He passed his hands over his eyes, and when he answered, it was in a shaky voice.

54
00:04:10.667 --> 00:04:16.155
"Know who you are? With your mother's eyes staring out of your face?

55
00:04:16.156 --> 00:04:18.856
Of course I know who you are.

56
00:04:18.857 --> 00:04:23.689
You're that poor sick boy with the crooked back and legs."

57
00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:27.151
Colin turned bright red and sat up straight.

58
00:04:27.152 --> 00:04:30.399
"I don't have a crooked back and legs."

59
00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:34.004
"He doesn't!" said Mary angrily.

60
00:04:34.005 --> 00:04:37.776
"I've looked, and he doesn't have any lumps on his back."

61
00:04:37.777 --> 00:04:40.503
Ben kept staring at Colin.

62
00:04:40.504 --> 00:04:45.099
"You don't have a crooked back? You don't have crooked legs?"

63
00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:47.592
This was too much for Colin.

64
00:04:47.593 --> 00:04:52.948
The energy that he usually threw into his tantrums rushed through him in a new way.

65
00:04:52.949 --> 00:04:57.246
His anger and insulted pride filled him with strength.

66
00:04:57.247 --> 00:05:02.938
"Stand by my side!" he ordered Dickon, throwing the blankets off his legs.

67
00:05:02.939 --> 00:05:07.703
Dickon held Colin's arm as Colin put his feet on the ground.

68
00:05:07.704 --> 00:05:10.958
"He can do it!" Mary whispered.

69
00:05:10.959 --> 00:05:14.708
Then Colin was standing as straight as an arrow.

70
00:05:14.709 --> 00:05:19.672
"Look at me!" he said to Ben. "Just you look at me!"

71
00:05:19.673 --> 00:05:23.424
"He's as straight as I am," said Dickon.

72
00:05:23.425 --> 00:05:26.950
Mary couldn't believe what Ben did next.

73
00:05:26.951 --> 00:05:32.066
He choked and gulped, and tears ran down his cheeks.

74
00:05:32.067 --> 00:05:38.783
"When my father is away," said Colin, "I am your master, and you must obey me.

75
00:05:38.784 --> 00:05:44.356
This is my garden. Don't you dare say a word about it.

76
00:05:44.357 --> 00:05:50.564
Get down from that ladder at once. Mary will meet you and bring you to us."

77
00:05:50.565 --> 00:05:56.785
"Yes, sir!" said Ben, and he climbed down the ladder.
